From 97860ebc214ba0b0ed2dca423c4c51c7deb265c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mita5358 <kafitcohr@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:08:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/20] feat(dsh): project only the newest history chunk when a
 session opens
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Opening a titan session (89k events, 447 MB of message and tool text) spent
~1.8 s in one projection pass over the whole record. The cost is the per-event
fold, not the row count: dropping the rows saved 34 ms, while rendering is
already bounded by MAX_ROWS.

So cut the translated record into round-aligned chunks (history-chunks.ts) and
project only the newest one for the first paint; a chunk opens on "load
earlier" and replaces its placeholder rows in place. A collapsed chunk keeps a
text digest row (rounds, events, tool calls, weight — no tool cards, no
terminal output, no command rows) plus one plain row per prompt, so the
timeline rail stays complete and its ticks land on real prompt text.

The tail keeps the small OPEN_* budget (its size is the open latency); older
pages take 50 rounds or ~1 MB of text, whichever comes first, and never split a
round. /tokens and the cost line still read the whole session: a row-less pass
folds the older chunks' request headers, model changes and usage into the same
buckets (124 ms on that record, tokens and cost identical to the full replay).
